\label{ch:ast}

The abstract syntax tree (AST) is the compiler's internal representation of the program's syntactic structure. It strips away the concrete syntax (parentheses, semicolons, whitespace) and retains only the essential hierarchical structure of the program. This chapter examines AST node types, memory representations, traversal patterns, and the role of ASTs in IDE support and incremental compilation.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 54.1
%% ============================================================
\section{AST Node Types and Structures}\label{sec:ast-nodes}

An AST is a tree where each node represents a syntactic construct. The node types correspond to the productions of the language's grammar.

\subsection{Node Classification}\label{sec:node-classification}

AST nodes can be classified into several categories:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Expressions.} Nodes that produce values: literals, variables, binary operations, function calls, lambdas.
  \item \textbf{Statements.} Nodes that perform actions: assignments, if/else, loops, return.
  \item \textbf{Declarations.} Nodes that introduce names: variable declarations, function declarations, class declarations.
  \item \textbf{Types.} Nodes that represent types: named types, function types, generic types.
  \item \textbf{Patterns.} Nodes that match values: literal patterns, variable patterns, constructor patterns.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{AST for a Simple Language}\label{sec:ast-simple}

\begin{grammar}{Simple Expression Language AST}
  \production{Expr}{\mathtt{Lit}(\mathrm{value}) \mid \mathtt{Var}(\mathrm{name}) \mid \mathtt{BinOp}(\mathrm{op}, \mathrm{lhs}, \mathrm{rhs}) \mid \mathtt{Call}(\mathrm{fn}, \mathrm{args}) \mid \mathtt{Lambda}(\mathrm{params}, \mathrm{body})} \\
  \production{Stmt}{\mathtt{Let}(\mathrm{name}, \mathrm{expr}) \mid \mathtt{If}(\mathrm{cond}, \mathrm{then}, \mathrm{else}) \mid \mathtt{While}(\mathrm{cond}, \mathrm{body}) \mid \mathtt{Return}(\mathrm{expr})} \\
  \production{Decl}{\mathtt{FnDecl}(\mathrm{name}, \mathrm{params}, \mathrm{retType}, \mathrm{body}) \mid \mathtt{VarDecl}(\mathrm{name}, \mathrm{type}, \mathrm{init})}
\end{grammar}

\subsection{AST with Annotations}\label{sec:ast-annotations}

In practice, AST nodes carry additional information beyond the syntactic structure:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Source location.} File name, line number, column number, for error reporting.
  \item \textbf{Type information.} The inferred or annotated type of each expression.
  \item \textbf{Symbol information.} Pointers to symbol table entries for name resolution.
  \item \textbf{Comments.} Associated comments for documentation generation.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  struct ExprNode {
    SourceLocation loc;     // source position
    Type type;              // inferred type (filled by type checker)
    Symbol symbol;          // resolved symbol (filled by name resolver)
    ExprKind kind;          // the syntactic kind
  };
\end{verbatim}

