\section{Memory Representation of ASTs}\label{sec:ast-memory}

The memory representation of ASTs affects traversal speed, memory usage, and cache performance.

\subsection{Pointer-Based Trees}\label{sec:pointer-trees}

The classical representation uses heap-allocated nodes connected by pointers:

\begin{verbatim}
  struct BinOpNode {
    Expr* lhs;
    Expr* rhs;
    Op op;
  };
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Advantages:} Flexible, easy to modify, supports sharing (DAGs).\\
\textbf{Disadvantages:} Poor cache locality (nodes scattered in memory), pointer overhead (8 bytes per pointer on 64-bit systems), allocation overhead.

\subsection{Arena Allocation}\label{sec:arena-allocation}

Arena (pool) allocation allocates all AST nodes from a contiguous memory region:

\begin{verbatim}
  class Arena {
    char* buffer;
    size_t offset;
    T* alloc<T>(args...) {
      T* node = (T*)(buffer + offset);
      offset += sizeof(T);
      return new(node) T(args...);
    }
  };
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Advantages:} Fast allocation (bump pointer), good cache locality, bulk deallocation.\\
\textbf{Disadvantages:} Cannot free individual nodes (must free the entire arena).

\subsection{Index-Based Representation}\label{sec:index-rep}

Instead of pointers, nodes are stored in arrays and referenced by index:

\begin{verbatim}
  struct BinOpNode {
    u32 lhs;    // index into expression array
    u32 rhs;    // index into expression array
    Op op;
  };
  Vec<ExprNode> expressions;
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Advantages:} Compact (4-byte indices vs. 8-byte pointers), serializable, supports interning.\\
\textbf{Disadvantages:} Indirect access (must index into array), less natural to manipulate.

\subsection{Interning and Hash-Consing}\label{sec:interning}

\emph{Interning} stores each unique AST node only once, returning the same reference for identical nodes. \emph{Hash-consing} uses a hash table to detect duplicates.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Hash-consed expression:
  Expr intern_lit(int value) {
    return hash_table.lookup_or_insert(Lit(value));
  }
  // intern_lit(42) always returns the same reference
\end{verbatim}

Interning enables $O(1)$ equality comparison (pointer equality) and reduces memory usage for programs with many identical subexpressions.

