\section{Traversal and Transformation Patterns}\label{sec:ast-traversal}

ASTs are traversed and transformed by a variety of patterns, each suited to different tasks.

\subsection{Visitor Pattern}\label{sec:visitor-pattern}

The \emph{visitor pattern} separates traversal from operation:

\begin{verbatim}
  interface ExprVisitor<R> {
    R visit_lit(LitNode node);
    R visit_var(VarNode node);
    R visit_binop(BinOpNode node);
    // ...
  }

  interface ExprNode {
    <R> R accept(ExprVisitor<R> visitor);
  }
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Advantages:} Clean separation, easy to add new operations.\\
\textbf{Disadvantages:} Adding new node types requires modifying all visitors; boilerplate-heavy.

\subsection{Generic Traversals}\label{sec:generic-traversals}

A \emph{generic traversal} applies a function to every node in the AST:

\begin{verbatim}
  fn map_expr(f: Expr -> Expr, e: Expr) -> Expr {
    match e {
      BinOp(op, lhs, rhs) => BinOp(op, map_expr(f, lhs), map_expr(f, rhs)),
      Call(fn, args) => Call(map_expr(f, fn), args.map(|a| map_expr(f, a))),
      other => f(other),
    }
  }
\end{verbatim}

Generic traversals can be:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Bottom-up.} Transform children before parents.
  \item \textbf{Top-down.} Transform parents before children.
  \item \textbf{Fold.} Accumulate a value while traversing.
  \item \textbf{Zip.} Traverse two ASTs in parallel.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Rewrite Rules}\label{sec:rewrite-rules}

Many compiler transformations are expressed as \emph{rewrite rules}: patterns that match a subtree and replace it with a new subtree.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Constant folding:
  rule BinOp(+, Lit(a), Lit(b)) => Lit(a + b)
  // Identity elimination:
  rule BinOp(+, x, Lit(0)) => x
  rule BinOp(*, x, Lit(1)) => x
\end{verbatim}

