\section{Visitor Pattern and Generic Traversals}\label{sec:visitor-generic}

\subsection{The Expression Problem}\label{sec:expression-problem}

The \emph{expression problem} (Wadler~\cite{wadler1998}) asks: can a design support both adding new node types and adding new operations, without modifying existing code?

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Visitor pattern.} Easy to add new operations; hard to add new node types.
  \item \textbf{Pattern matching (functional).} Easy to add new node types; hard to add new operations.
  \item \textbf{Data types a la carte.} Supports both, using type classes to compose node types.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Scrap Your Boilerplate}\label{sec:scrap-boilerplate}

Generic programming techniques (e.g., Haskell's \emph{Scrap Your Boilerplate}~\cite{lohmuller2024}) allow writing traversals that work uniformly over any AST type, without boilerplate for each node type.

\subsection{Strategic Programming}\label{sec:strategic-programming}

\emph{Strategic programming} (Visser~\cite{visser2004}) combines rewrite rules with traversal strategies:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Apply constant folding everywhere in the AST:
  bottomup(constant_fold)
\end{verbatim}

Strategies compose: \texttt{try}, \texttt{repeat}, \texttt{sequence}, \texttt{choice}.

