\section{Incremental AST Updates}\label{sec:incremental-ast}

IDEs and incremental compilers need to update the AST efficiently when the source code changes.

\subsection{Rope-Based Source Representation}\label{sec:rope-source}

A \emph{rope} is a tree-based data structure for representing strings that supports efficient insertion, deletion, and concatenation. The source code is stored as a rope, and the AST references spans of the rope.

\subsection{Incremental Parsing}\label{sec:incremental-parsing}

When the source changes, only the affected portion of the AST needs to be reparsed:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the changed region of the source.
  \item Find the AST nodes that overlap the changed region.
  \item Reparse only those nodes.
  \item Splice the new nodes into the existing AST.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Tree-Sitter}\label{sec:tree-sitter}

Tree-sitter is an incremental parsing framework that maintains a concrete syntax tree (CST) that is updated incrementally as the source changes. It uses GLR parsing with error recovery, enabling parsing of incomplete or invalid code.

\begin{insight}
Incremental AST updates are essential for IDE responsiveness. A full reparse of a large codebase can take seconds; an incremental update takes milliseconds. The key insight is that most edits affect only a small region of the code, and the AST can be updated locally without disturbing the rest.
\end{insight}

