\section{ASTs for IDE Support}\label{sec:ast-ide}

Modern IDEs rely heavily on ASTs for features like code completion, refactoring, and semantic highlighting.

\subsection{Semantic Analysis}\label{sec:semantic-analysis}

The AST, annotated with type and symbol information, enables:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Go to definition.} Follow the symbol reference to its declaration.
  \item \textbf{Find references.} Search the AST for all uses of a symbol.
  \item \textbf{Code completion.} Use the type of the current expression to suggest completions.
  \item \textbf{Semantic highlighting.} Color identifiers based on their role (variable, type, function).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Refactoring}\label{sec:refactoring}

Refactoring operations (rename, extract method, inline variable) are implemented as AST transformations:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the AST node(s) to be refactored.
  \item Compute the transformation (e.g., rename all references to a symbol).
  \item Apply the transformation to the AST.
  \item Regenerate the source code from the modified AST.
\end{enumerate}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Checking an AST Rename for Accidental Capture}

An editor rename is not a textual replacement. Use the resolved
AST to select a declaration by its symbol identity, then prepare
edits for that declaration and references bound to it. Even if
all currently bound references are found, the new spelling could
capture a reference that used to name a different declaration.
For a simple lexical language, check the proposal on a scratch
AST before producing source edits. Assume declaration identities
and reference occurrence IDs survive the scratch edit, and start
with a well-resolved program. Imports, macro expansion and
overloaded names are outside this fragment.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Resolved AST $A$, symbol $s$, legal new identifier $n$
\State $R\gets$ map of reference IDs to declarations in $A$
\State $A'\gets$ scratch copy of $A$
\State change declaration $s$ to $n$ in $A'$
\ForAll{references $r$ with $R[r]=s$}
  \State change $r$ to $n$ in $A'$ \Comment{Select by binding, not text}
\EndFor
\State $R'\gets$ resolve $A'$; reject any binding error
\ForAll{reference IDs $r$ in $R$}
  \If{$R'[r]\ne R[r]$}
    \State \Return reject and show $r$ \Comment{Check untouched uses too}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return source edits for the changed spellings
\end{sudocode}

Consider \texttt{let x = 1 in (let y = 2 in x + y)}.
Renaming the outer \texttt{x} to \texttt{y} tentatively produces
an inner sum \texttt{y + y}. Its first reference now resolves
to the inner binder, so the procedure rejects the change.
Renaming to \texttt{total} keeps both identities and succeeds,
leaving the sum equal to 3. In the opposite direction, renaming
an inner binder to an outer name could capture a reference the
procedure did not itself edit; comparing every entry detects that too.

On success, the declaration map is unchanged at every reference
occurrence. Identifiers may have different source spans, so compare
stable logical IDs rather than raw byte offsets. A scratch pass adds
one full resolution and one reference-map comparison; an incremental
editor can limit that to affected scopes only if it also tracks
references whose lookup crosses those scopes. Apply the result to
the version that was checked, or recompute it after further edits.

\subsection{Language Server Protocol}\label{sec:lsp}

The Language Server Protocol (LSP) standardizes the communication between IDEs and language servers. The language server maintains the AST and provides services like completion, diagnostics, and hover information.

