\begin{historical}
ASTs have been central to compiler design since the development of the first optimizing compilers in the 1960s. The term ``abstract syntax tree'' was coined to distinguish the tree representation from the concrete syntax (parse tree). The visitor pattern, popularized by the Gang of Four~\cite{gamma1994}, became the standard way to traverse ASTs in object-oriented languages. The rise of IDEs in the 2000s (Eclipse, IntelliJ) drove the development of incremental parsing and AST manipulation frameworks. Modern tools like Tree-sitter, Roslyn, and the Rust Analyzer have pushed the boundaries of IDE-quality parsing and analysis.
\end{historical}

