\section{Advanced Traversal Patterns and Incremental Updates}\label{sec:ast-traversal-advanced}

\subsection{Fold and Map Patterns}\label{sec:fold-map}

The two fundamental AST traversal patterns are \emph{fold} (reduce) and \emph{map} (transform):

\begin{verbatim}
  // Fold: accumulate a value while traversing
  fn fold_expr<B>(f: (B, &Expr) -> B, init: B, e: &Expr) -> B {
    let acc = f(init, e);
    match e {
      BinOp(_, lhs, rhs) => {
        let acc = fold_expr(f, acc, lhs);
        fold_expr(f, acc, rhs)
      }
      Call(func, args) => {
        let acc = fold_expr(f, acc, func);
        args.iter().fold(acc, |a, arg| fold_expr(f, a, arg))
      }
      _ => acc,
    }
  }

  // Map: transform each node
  fn map_expr(f: &dyn Fn(Expr) -> Expr, e: Expr) -> Expr {
    let e = match e {
      BinOp(op, lhs, rhs) => BinOp(op, map_expr(f, lhs), map_expr(f, rhs)),
      Call(func, args) => Call(map_expr(f, func),
                                args.into_iter().map(|a| map_expr(f, a)).collect()),
      other => other,
    };
    f(e)  // apply transformation at this node
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Attribute Grammars}\label{sec:attribute-grammars}

\emph{Attribute grammars} provide a declarative framework for AST traversal by associating \emph{attributes} with each node:

\begin{definition}[Attribute Grammar]\label{def:attribute-grammar}
An \emph{attribute grammar} extends a context-free grammar with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Synthesized attributes.} Computed from children to parent (bottom-up).
  \item \textbf{Inherited attributes.} Computed from parent to children (top-down).
\end{itemize}
Each attribute is defined by an equation in terms of other attributes.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Type checking as attribute grammar]\label{ex:attr-type-check}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Synthesized attribute: type of each expression
  Expr.type : Type

  Lit(n).type = Int
  Var(x).type = env.lookup(x)
  BinOp(+, lhs, rhs).type = Int  // requires lhs.type = rhs.type = Int
  Call(f, args).type = f.type.return_type

  // Inherited attribute: environment
  Expr.env : Environment
  Block(stmts).env = parent_env
  Let(x, e, body).env[x] = e.type  // extend environment
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Incremental AST Updates in Detail}\label{sec:incremental-detail}

Modern IDEs maintain a persistent AST that is updated incrementally as the user edits the source code.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Incremental AST Update}\label{alg:incremental-ast}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{IncrementalUpdate}{$\mathrm{AST}, \mathrm{edit}$}
  \State $\mathrm{region} \gets \mathrm{affected\_region}(\mathrm{edit})$
  \State $\mathrm{nodes} \gets \mathrm{find\_overlapping}(\mathrm{AST}, \mathrm{region})$
  \State $\mathrm{parent} \gets \mathrm{lowest\_common\_ancestor}(\mathrm{nodes})$
  \State $\mathrm{new\_source} \gets \mathrm{apply\_edit}(\mathrm{source}, \mathrm{edit})$
  \State $\mathrm{new\_subtree} \gets \mathrm{reparse}(\mathrm{new\_source}[\mathrm{parent.span}])$
  \State $\mathrm{AST}.\mathrm{replace}(\mathrm{parent}, \mathrm{new\_subtree})$
  \State \Comment{Update annotations for affected nodes}
  \State $\mathrm{reanalyze}(\mathrm{AST}, \mathrm{affected\_nodes})$
  \State \Return $\mathrm{AST}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Tree-Sitter's Incremental Parsing}\label{sec:tree-sitter-detail}

Tree-sitter uses an incremental GLR parser that can handle ambiguous grammars and recover from errors:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Error recovery.} When parsing fails, Tree-sitter inserts an \texttt{ERROR} node and continues parsing, producing a tree even for incomplete or invalid code.
  \item \textbf{Incremental updates.} When the source changes, Tree-sitter identifies the affected region and re-parses only that portion, reusing the rest of the tree.
  \item \textbf{Ambiguity handling.} Tree-sitter uses GLR parsing to handle ambiguous grammars, maintaining multiple parse trees until disambiguation.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Tree-sitter edit workflow:
  let mut parser = Parser::new();
  parser.set_language(&language()).unwrap();

  // Initial parse:
  let mut tree = parser.parse(source, None).unwrap();

  // After edit:
  let edit = InputEdit { start_byte, old_end_byte, new_end_byte, ... };
  tree.edit(&edit);
  // Re-parse only the affected region:
  let new_tree = parser.parse(new_source, Some(&tree)).unwrap();
  // Time: O(changed region) instead of O(entire file)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{AST Serialization and Caching}\label{sec:ast-serialization}

Compilers cache parsed ASTs to avoid re-parsing unchanged files:

\begin{verbatim}
  // AST serialization format:
  struct SerializedAST {
    version: u32,
    checksum: u64,           // hash of source file
    nodes: Vec<SerializedNode>,  // flat array of nodes
    // Each node: (kind, children_offset, children_count, data)
  }

  // Cache lookup:
  // 1. Compute checksum of source file
  // 2. Look up cached AST by checksum
  // 3. If found, deserialize and reuse
  // 4. If not found, parse from scratch and cache
\end{verbatim}

