\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-54}

The AST is the compiler's primary representation of the program's structure. Its design---node types, memory representation, traversal patterns---affects every phase of compilation. Arena allocation and index-based representations improve performance; visitor patterns and generic traversals provide flexibility; incremental updates enable IDE responsiveness.

For further reading, we recommend Appel's treatment of tree representations~\cite{appel1992}, the Tree-sitter documentation~\cite{treesitter2024}, and the Roslyn compiler's architecture~\cite{roslyn2024}.
